# AC-1 -- the M4 loopback on real hardware, driven from the laptop over SSH.
# robot.service stopped first (it holds 127.0.0.1:8787 and the ALSA capture device).

$ cd /opt/avid && .venv/bin/python -u docs/demos/audio_pi.py \
      --mode loopback --config /etc/robot/config.toml --turns 3
Speak 3 short phrases (pause between each); listening...
turn       round-trip     played    elapsed
------ -------------- ---------- ----------
1             0.14 ms     860 ms     770 ms
2             0.14 ms    1140 ms    1047 ms
3             0.12 ms     900 ms     812 ms
------ -------------- ---------- ----------
min 0.12 ms / median 0.14 ms / max 0.14 ms / count 3
PASS: all 3 turns within the 200 ms turnaround budget
      playback integrity: 3/3 turns, worst divergence 10.5%   (speaker=alsa)

# The "(speaker=alsa)" tag matters: it proves the playback-integrity check was ARMED.
# Behind a fake speaker the run prints "playback integrity: NOT CHECKED" instead, because
# a check that disarms itself silently is the same defect that let a mute robot pass.


# The same loop, instrumented to report the LEVEL of each echo rather than only its
# duration -- so a silent echo (silence went in) is distinguishable from a dropped one
# (audio went in, nothing came out). This distinction resolved a false alarm: earlier
# "silent" echoes were the Logitech H540's HARDWARE boom mute feeding digital silence
# into the loopback (224000 frames of exact zero), not a playback defect.

$ cd /opt/avid && .venv/bin/python -u /tmp/gate2.py
SPEAK 3 short phrases, clearly, pausing ~1s between each...
    [INFO avid.adapters.speaker] speaker opened at 16000 Hz / 1ch; nominal format is 24000 Hz / 1ch (honouring the chunk)

turn    captured    rate    played   elapsed   verdict
--------------------------------------------------------------
1       -14.7dB   16000   1140 ms   1051 ms   good
2        -4.0dB   16000   1160 ms   1072 ms   good
3        -6.0dB   16000   1340 ms   1243 ms   good

# captured: real speech at -14.7 / -4.0 / -6.0 dBFS, not silence.
# rate 16000 on every turn: the device was reopened at the ECHO's rate, not the
#   configured nominal 24 kHz. The INFO line fired exactly ONCE, as designed -- the
#   loopback deviates on every turn, so logging each one would bury the signal.
# played vs elapsed: short by 89 / 88 / 97 ms. Six measurements across two runs all
#   cluster at ~90 ms = the ring-buffer depth, not noise.
# Three consecutive turns all played. On the old adapter turn 2 would have been silent
#   (see ab_old_vs_new.log). No dropped-period warnings were logged.
# Echo confirmed by ear by the operator: all three returned, at natural pitch and speed.
